  "response": "**Setting up the calculation (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prevalence: P(disease) = 1/500 = 0.002\n- Sensitivity: P(test+ | disease) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(test− | no disease) = 0.95, so P(test+ | no disease) = 0.05\n\n**True positives (per population unit):**\nP(disease) × sensitivity = 0.002 × 0.98 = 0.00196\n\n**False positives:**\nP(no disease) × (1 − specificity) = 0.998 × 0.05 = 0.0499\n\n**Total positives:**\n0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\n**Positive predictive value:**\n0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794 → **3.78%**\n\nSo even with a positive test result, there's only about a 3.78% chance the person actually has the condition — a consequence of the low base rate (1/500) combined with a 5% false-positive rate that generates far more false positives than true positives.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
